-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Ensure normal vectors are normalized after updates to maintain unit length [G].
-*   **Boundaries:** Handle edge pixels in rendering to avoid artifacts during loss calculation [G].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If volume data is sparse, Stage 1 may require more iterations to capture geometry accurately [G].
 ## Subtopics
 
-### iVR-GS Two-Stage Training
-It explicitly separates the training into a 30,000-iteration base 3DGS phase and a 10,000-iteration editable Gaussian phase [S1]. It is chosen when computational efficiency and reduced model size are priorities [S1]. Compared to one-stage training (40,000 iterations from scratch), it accelerates optimization, uses fewer primitives, and reduces training time by over 2× while maintaining similar reconstruction quality [S1].
+### Composability of iVR-GS Models
+A feature allowing multiple independently trained iVR-GS models (basic scenes) to be combined by simply concatenating their Gaussian parameters [S2]. To use this, train one basic iVR-GS model for each segmented component in the scene, then combine models by concatenating their Gaussian parameters to reconstruct the complete VolVis scene or view individual components [S2]. This enables users to view each semantic component individually or reconstruct the complete scene [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The two-stage approach reduces total training time by over 2× compared to one-stage training [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses fewer primitives than one-stage training, resulting in smaller file sizes (e.g., 8.8 MB vs 16.7 MB for one basic scene in Table 2 of [S1]).
-*   **Performance:** Maintains similar PSNR and LPIPS metrics to one-stage training [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Faster convergence, smaller model size, efficient use of primitives [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful initialization of editable Gaussians on the base geometry [G].
-*   **When to Use:** When training time and model size are critical constraints [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** If maximum possible reconstruction quality is required regardless of resource cost, one-stage training might be considered, though iVR-GS claims comparable quality [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Two-stage training requires 40,000 total iterations (30,000 base + 10,000 editable) [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on scene size and resolution. For example, on a $1024 \times 1024 \times 1080$ volume, CPU/GPU memory usage is 12.3 GB/7.1 GB [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Rendering time varies by scene size; e.g., 361 ms for the largest tested volume ($2048 \times 2048 \times 2048$) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Decoupled training improves stability and convergence speed compared to joint optimization [S1]. Supports composability for modular scene editing [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires semantic segmentation for composability [S2]. Two-stage process adds complexity to the training pipeline [S1].
 
 ## Background
-iVR-GS was introduced in the paper "iVR-GS: Inverse Volume Rendering for Explorable Visualization via Editable 3D Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. The method leverages the efficiency of 3D Gaussian Splatting for volume rendering and introduces a two-stage training strategy to enable interactive editing of volume visualizations [S1]. It builds upon the base 3DGS optimization framework to provide a robust geometric foundation before refining shading attributes [S1].
+iVR-GS Training was introduced in the iVR-GS paper [S1] and further applied in the NLI4VolVis framework [S2]. It builds upon the original 3DGS optimization method [S1] by adding editable shading attributes and a two-stage training strategy to handle volume visualization tasks [S1, S2]. The method is designed for explorable visualization via editable 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1].
 
